"use strict";

/* METHOD CHAPTERS - the manual's prose, split out of method.js when that module reached the
   70KB ceiling. This file is content; method.js is the machinery that renders it, remembers
   which chapters are open and builds the contents list. Nothing here runs at load time, so
   it can sit either side of its renderer in the script order. */

function methodChapters(){
  return [
    {id:"method-start",title:"Start here",summary:"The operating loop, cash versus BTC, the operating bill and how to read your ledger.",lead:"Mining earns BTC. Running the operation costs cash. Those are two separate balances, and BTC has to be sold before it can pay for electricity. At each month boundary the recurring costs you have accrued are settled from liquid cash - that settlement is the operating bill. Most runs end because the cash ran out, not because the mining stopped.",body:`<h3 id="method-no-market">Before Bitcoin has a market</h3><p>Bitcoin has no market price until <strong>July 2010</strong>. Until then there is nowhere to sell what you mine, so the coins pile up and <strong>cash is the only thing that pays the bill</strong>. That is why a run opens with <strong>$2,500</strong> by default: it has to carry the laptop through the first eighteen months, until the first exchange opens.</p><p>The bill is paid from cash or by selling miners, and you can sell any machine you have bought, the installed ones included, at its resale value. There is no other rescue before the market opens: you cannot restructure, carry the bill into arrears or borrow. If the cash and the sale of your miners together fall short of a bill, <strong>the scored campaign ends</strong>. The Dashboard warns you when your cash will not last until the market opens, so the choice that matters is how much hardware you can afford to keep paying for. A campaign that starts after July 2010 does not have this gap.</p><h3 id="method-offline">Putting a run on hold: cutting the internet</h3><p>The internet is the one recurring cost you can stop. In <strong>Facilities</strong> the line is a plan like any other, and <strong>No internet (cut the line)</strong> costs nothing and lets nothing mine. The clock keeps running. It is how you hibernate: <strong>stop the machines</strong> (the laptop included, since anything left switched on keeps drawing power), <strong>sell what is spare</strong>, cut the line, and wait. Rent, staff and insurance still fall due, so the bill is not always zero, and the Dashboard says exactly what is still being charged.</p><p>The price of the quiet is that you cannot see anything. While the line is cut the Dashboard becomes a summary of what you are paying, the Mine keeps the floor, servicing and cooling (the catalogue is locked), and the Ledger and this manual stay open; Pools, Treasury, Facilities, Energy, Learn and Tech are locked, the ticker's outside numbers read NO LINK and the Bitcoin story goes quiet. The major chapters of the story that happen meanwhile, such as Bitcoin getting its first price in July 2010, are held and shown one at a time when you reconnect; what they do to your operation still happens on the day. So the real decision is when to come back, and nothing tells you.</p><h3>Clock, story and simulation speed</h3><p>One simulation day advances per tick. The story feed progresses through dated chapters; Story pause stops the clock at major chapters, and entering Mine pauses time while you shop for hardware, restoring your selected speed on exit. After the historical data cutoff, Sandbox continuation keeps the operation running for up to 100 further simulated years. Price, network hash rate, difficulty, transaction fees and block height all continue on a procedural model - deterministic and bounded, not a forecast - while halvings keep landing on schedule exactly as they always have; hardware and story events don't gain any new entries, since nothing real exists to add. The continuation itself ends at that 100-year mark.</p><h3 id="method-reference">Quick reference</h3><h4 id="method-tour">The guided tour</h4><p>A new run begins with a walk round every area of the game: the clock, the numbers across the top, your briefing, the history, Mine, Pools, the Treasury with its Market, Custody and Finance sections, Facilities, Energy, Learn, Tech, the Ledger and this manual. Each stop opens the page, rings the part that matters and says in a few sentences what it is for and what you can do there. The clock is stopped for the whole walk and put back as it was when it ends, so nothing can go wrong while you read. You can leave at any step, step back, or use the arrow keys, and you can take it again at any time from <b>Tour</b> in the footer. A save from before the tour existed is not walked through it. What to do next, in the moment, is the briefing on the Dashboard, which carries on after the tour has ended.</p><h4 id="method-ledger">Reading the ledger</h4><p>Mining income is written once per simulated day, not once per block: a solo entry reads <code>Block reward (today)</code> - or <code>Block reward ×N (today)</code> when more than one block landed - so the figure is that day's total subsidy plus fees, never a single block's reward. Pool mining logs the same daily total as a pool payout.</p><h4>Asset map and live header</h4><p>The header is the live asset snapshot: <strong>Liquid fiat</strong> is spendable cash; <strong>Illiquid fiat</strong> is ETF plus Strategy equity value that must be sold before it can pay a bill; <strong>Self-held BTC</strong> is hot plus cold bitcoin under your keys; <strong>Custodial BTC</strong> is exchange or frozen-claim exposure; and <strong>Lightning locked</strong> is hot BTC committed to routing channels. Every satoshi you still hold at the end counts toward the final score the same way, whether it sits in self-held keys, on an exchange, in a frozen claim or locked in a Lightning channel.</p><table><tr><th>Asset</th><th>What it means</th><th>Can it pay a bill? / Operator Score</th></tr><tr><td>Liquid fiat</td><td>Cash available for bills, miners and market orders</td><td>Spendable; supports reserves</td></tr><tr><td>Illiquid fiat</td><td>ETF and Strategy equity market value</td><td>Sell first to pay a bill</td></tr><tr><td>Self-held BTC</td><td>Hot and cold wallet bitcoin under your keys</td><td>Spendable now; included in final score</td></tr><tr><td>Custodial BTC</td><td>Exchange and frozen-claim balances</td><td>Venue-dependent; included in final score</td></tr><tr><td>Lightning locked BTC</td><td>Hot BTC committed to routing channels</td><td>Withdraw before spending; included in final score</td></tr></table><h4>Strategy equities</h4><p>Strategy securities are optional, fiat-priced capital-structure exposure to Strategy's Bitcoin-treasury business - not Bitcoin, not self-custody, not liquid fiat. Their game price starts from a model price and moves with Bitcoin from the security's unlock date using its BTC sensitivity: a gameplay proxy, not a recreation of historical security prices.</p><table><tr><th>Ticker</th><th>In-game exposure</th><th>Modelled fiat income</th></tr><tr><td>MSTR</td><td>Common equity; 1.65× BTC sensitivity</td><td>None</td></tr><tr><td>STRK</td><td>Strike preferred; 0.35× BTC sensitivity</td><td>8% annual accrual</td></tr><tr><td>STRF</td><td>Strife preferred; 0.20× BTC sensitivity</td><td>10% annual accrual</td></tr><tr><td>STRD</td><td>Stride preferred; 0.25× BTC sensitivity</td><td>10% annual accrual</td></tr><tr><td>STRC</td><td>Stretch preferred; 0.08× BTC sensitivity</td><td>9% annual accrual</td></tr></table><p>Buy and sell Strategy shares in Market using liquid fiat. They appear in Finance as illiquid fiat and count toward net worth, but can't pay a bill until sold; every preferred position uses a simplified daily fiat accrual at its displayed annual rate, not a real-world dividend guarantee.</p><h4>Starting difficulty and Starting Liquidity</h4><p>Difficulty moves the campaign start date, while Starting Liquidity sets the opening liquid cash from a $2,500 default (the minimum is $1,500) up to the current $1,500,000 maximum. Easy begins at the Genesis Block itself, 03 January 2009; Standard opens a month later, on 03 February 2009; Hard starts 29 January 2013, as the first ASICs ship and CPU, GPU and FPGA hardware is already obsolete for competitive mining; and Impossible starts 01 January 2025, after the fourth halving with under 20 months of historical record left before the campaign end date. It changes nothing else: not historical prices, network data, hardware dates, incident rules or scoring weights, and the selected mode is stored permanently in the activity ledger.</p><h4 id="method-spending">Spending it</h4><p>For years the honest answer to what you could buy with bitcoin was: a gift card. Not a payment terminal at the till - a website that took your coins and handed back a code for a shop that had never heard of bitcoin and did not need to. Gyft did it from 2013, eGifter followed during the first boom, Bitrefill grew a business out of it, and prepaid top-ups arrived later as the closest thing to simply spending it.</p><p>The Market section of the Treasury lets you do the same. The coins leave your hot wallet at the day's price, a code arrives, and the only thing the operation gains is <strong>operator experience</strong> - no cash, no equipment, no advantage. Experience scales with what you spent and has heavy diminishing returns, so a twenty-five dollar card teaches most of what a five-thousand dollar one does; buying one enormous card is not a strategy.</p><p>The desk keeps a running note of what everything you have spent would be worth today. That number is the entire reason anyone still talks about a pizza, and it is the only reward the feature is really offering.</p><h4>Transaction sizing and procurement</h4><p>Percentage-sized actions use the named source balance at review time: liquid cash for buys, the selected BTC wallet for sells and transfers, or the held security position for security sales. Miner orders are whole units, capped by cash or hot BTC, facility power, floor space and outstanding reserved capacity; opening a confirmation pauses the simulation, so execution uses the same figures shown in the review. Miner purchases are prepaid but can slip on modelled lead times; delivered equipment stays staged until commissioned. At each month boundary the operation settles electricity, rent, internet, staff, insurance, node services and finance interest together - a shortfall pauses time for an explicit sale, liquidation, bridge-finance or receivership decision.</p><h4 id="method-solvency">What counts as a solvent month</h4><p>A bill met from operating cash is a solvent month. A bill met by selling your own bitcoin is not - and the distinction is the difference between a business and a slow liquidation. Both keep the lights on; only one of them is the operation paying for itself.</p><p>This used to be scored the same either way. An operation funding every bill by selling a thousand bitcoin recorded twenty-five solvent months out of twenty-five, exactly like one that had earned the money, and the treasury policy did it automatically without so much as a prompt. Selling to cover now records the month as unsolvent, whether you sold by hand or the policy did it for you, and the ledger line says so at the time.</p><p>It changes nothing about survival. You can still fund an operation out of a large enough treasury for years, because you genuinely can. What changes is that the score stops calling it success.</p><h4>Operator Score</h4><p>Up to 740 points come from era performance: bills paid, realised mining profitability, uptime and keeping a competitive fleet, and a further 60 from operator mastery - your peak level, capped at level 50. Milestones and total BTC holdings - self-held, custodial and Lightning-locked combined - contribute up to 120 points. Cash reserves and resilience against bridge loans or receivership contribute the final 80. Starting with more cash makes survival easier but does not directly change these weights.</p><h4>Where the historical data comes from</h4><p>${(()=>{const meta=(window.HISTORICAL_DATA||{}).meta;if(!meta)return "The recorded series are bundled with the build.";const counts=(window.HISTORICAL_DATA?[["PRICE","BTC/USD price"],["DIFFICULTY","difficulty"],["HASH","network hash rate"],["FEES","transaction fees"],["TX","transaction count"],["HEIGHT","block height"],["CAP","market capitalisation"]].filter(([key])=>Array.isArray(window.HISTORICAL_DATA[key])).map(([key,label])=>`${fmtNum(window.HISTORICAL_DATA[key].length)} ${label}`).join(", "):"");return `Every recorded series in this game is pulled from <strong>${meta.source}</strong> and regenerated by a script in the repository rather than typed in by hand - the bundled file carries ${counts}, current through ${dateFmt(at(meta.through),true)}. Nothing about the market, the network or the chain is invented.`})()}</p><table><tr><th>Series</th><th>How it was derived</th></tr>${Object.entries(((window.HISTORICAL_DATA||{}).meta||{transforms:{}}).transforms||{}).map(([key,how])=>`<tr><td>${key}</td><td>${how}</td></tr>`).join("")}</table><p class="modal-note">Where a series is sampled weekly rather than daily, values between samples are interpolated, and the tab labels that as ${sourceTag("derived")} rather than ${sourceTag("recorded")}. Pool market shares are separate: early history is smoothed, and 2024 onward uses weekly block-attribution observations from mempool.space. Hardware prices, incident rates, uptime, repair behaviour and every outcome in the game are ${sourceTag("modelled")} - they are not in the dataset and are not claimed to be.</p><h4>What is historical vs modelled</h4><table><tr><th>Class</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Examples</th></tr><tr><td>${sourceTag("recorded")}</td><td>Historical anchor</td><td>Dates, price points, halvings, known events</td></tr><tr><td>${sourceTag("derived")}</td><td>Calculated from anchors and your state</td><td>Network share, expected yield, bills, net worth</td></tr><tr><td>${sourceTag("modelled")}</td><td>Gameplay simplification</td><td>Hardware prices and resale, incidents, outages, venue freezes, Lightning fees, part-fault attribution, repair complications, overdrive risk multipliers, venue bid/ask spreads, order-book depth and trade impact</td></tr></table><p class="modal-note">The purpose is to expose the economic relationships that come with running a Bitcoin operation through changing eras - not to predict Bitcoin prices or promise investment returns.</p></article><h3 id="method-saves">Where your game is kept</h3><p>Your run is saved in this browser's local storage, after every simulated day, and nowhere else. Nothing is sent anywhere: the game makes no network calls once the page has loaded, collects no analytics and has no accounts. The header reads <strong>LOCAL SAVE</strong> while that is working. Clearing site data, using another browser or a private window starts you from nothing, so <strong>Export save</strong> in the footer is how you keep a copy or move to another device, and <strong>Import save</strong> is how you bring it back.</p><p>If the browser refuses to store the game (a private window in some browsers, blocked site data, a full disk) the header reads <strong>NOT SAVING</strong> and you are told once. Export before you close the tab.</p><h3 id="method-saves-unreadable">A save that cannot be read</h3><p>If the stored save is damaged, or does not look like a save this game made, the game does not overwrite it. It keeps the raw text under a separate key, starts a fresh run and says so. <strong>Export unreadable save</strong> in the footer downloads that text so it can be looked at or repaired. If the game cannot start at all, a plain recovery page offers <strong>Export my save</strong>, <strong>Start a new run</strong> (which sets the old save aside the same way) and <strong>Try again</strong>, with the technical details to copy into a report.</p><h3 id="method-saves-tab">The clock and the tab</h3><p>The game pauses when the tab is hidden and does not resume by itself: the clock keeps the speed you had and waits for you to press play. Nothing happens to your operation while you are away, so a bill never falls due unseen.</p><h3 id="method-saves-key">The key from the first ceremony</h3><p>The key your first wallet is made from comes from your browser's own random number generator, whether you roll the dice or let the game do it. It is for this game only. It is kept unencrypted with the rest of your save, so never send real bitcoin to anything derived from it.</p><h3 id="method-saves-feedback">Reporting a problem</h3><p><strong>Report a bug</strong> and <strong>Suggest something</strong> in the footer open a GitHub form with this version and the in-game date already filled in. <strong>Copy debug info</strong> puts the version, in-game date, browser, window size and any error on your clipboard, with none of your save in it, to paste into a report.</p>`},
    {id:"method-rewards",title:"Mining and rewards",summary:"Hash rate, network difficulty, subsidy, transaction fees, solo variance and pool schemes.",lead:"Your machines compete for blocks against the entire network. How often you win depends on your share of the total work being done; what a win pays depends on the block subsidy, the fees in the block, and whether you mine solo or through a pool. This chapter makes both of those precise.",body:`<h3 id="method-mining">Mining probability and payout</h3><p>How often you win comes down to one number: your share of all the work being done. Add hash rate and your share rises, but so does the total you are dividing into, so a bigger fleet never earns quite proportionally more. Downtime costs you directly - a machine that is not running is not entered in the draw at all.</p><details class="method-detail"><summary>Exact calculation</summary><p>Your effective share is <code>active fleet hash ÷ (effective competitive hash + active fleet hash)</code>. Effective competitive hash is not simply the recorded historical network hash: it is the larger of that historical baseline and a modelled floor for unseen miners, plus a scaling response to the size of your own fleet - buying hardware does not just divide a fixed pie. Expected blocks per day is <code>144 × share × uptime</code>, where uptime is regional grid reliability, capped at 100%, plus a small Monitoring bonus. Expected BTC per day multiplies those blocks by the current subsidy plus the modelled transaction-fee component.</p></details><p><strong>The early years are deliberately damped, and by a large factor.</strong> Run the honest formula on February 2009 and one laptop at 5 MH/s against difficulty 1 finds about 100 blocks a day - roughly 5,000 BTC - because that is genuinely what the arithmetic said when almost nobody was hashing. A campaign that hands you that in week one is over before it starts, so the modelled competition is scaled up early and tapered away. From the ASIC era onward the simulation is running the real arithmetic; before it, it is not, and that is a game-balance decision rather than a historical claim. At your current fleet and date the factor is <strong>${(yieldDampingAt(state.time,fleet().hash)*100).toFixed(0)}%</strong> of the unmodified rate.</p><details class="method-detail"><summary>The damping factor, year by year</summary><p>Modelled competition is scaled by about 60× in 2009, 40× in 2010, 12× in 2011, 4× in 2012, 1.5× in 2013 and 1× from 2014 onward. Measured against the unmodified formula, the effect on your yield is roughly <strong>0.02× in early 2009, 0.04× in mid-2010, 0.11× in 2011, 0.69× in 2013, 0.92× in 2017 and 0.99× from 2024</strong>.</p></details><table><tr><th>Mechanic</th><th>Solo</th><th>Pool</th></tr><tr><td>Underlying odds</td><td>Your share of block-finding probability</td><td>Same expected share</td></tr><tr><td>Variance</td><td>High: whole blocks arrive randomly</td><td>Low: rewards are smoothed</td></tr><tr><td>Fee</td><td>None</td><td>${(poolFee()*100).toFixed(2)}% currently · ${POOL_SCHEMES[poolScheme()].name}</td></tr><tr><td>Reward timing</td><td>Can be very long between wins</td><td>Small, regular simulated payouts</td></tr></table><p><strong>Does your own hash rate move difficulty?</strong> Yes, and the share formula is where it happens. Your odds are <code>your hash ÷ (competition + your hash)</code>, so the hash you add sits in your own denominator exactly as it would sit in the network total that difficulty retargets against. The practical consequence matches the real thing: below about 1% of the network, doubling your fleet almost exactly doubles your blocks, because you barely move the total. Past that it visibly bites - at roughly 2.5% of network hash, per-machine yield is about 12% lower than the same machine would earn in a small fleet. What the game does <em>not</em> do is rewrite recorded history: the displayed difficulty is the real reconstructed series for that date, not a counterfactual in which your fleet also existed.</p><p><strong>Your best share.</strong> Every hash your fleet completes is a lottery ticket, and the difficulty of the best one is the number miners actually watch. Your record climbs in rare jumps rather than smoothly, and it is reconciled with what the day actually produced rather than tracked separately.</p><details class="method-detail"><summary>Exact calculation</summary><p>The chance of a share reaching difficulty <em>d</em> is <code>1/d</code>, so the day's best share is heavy-tailed. Because a share at or above the network difficulty <em>is</em> a valid block, mining solo your best share crosses network difficulty on exactly the days you find a block and never on the days you do not. Pool mining, a share above network difficulty is you finding the block on the pool's behalf, which the Ledger names.</p></details><p>Solo mining draws a seeded Poisson process, so a small miner can go a long time between blocks and then find several in a row. Pool mining smooths the same expected economics, less the selected pool's fee; each historical pool carries its own approximate market share and base fee, and the Operations skill tree can trim that fee by up to 0.4 percentage points.</p><p><strong>The payout scheme matters as much as the headline fee</strong>. <strong>FPPS</strong> pays a flat rate per share covering the subsidy <em>and</em> an average of transaction fees, whether or not the pool finds a block - the pool absorbs the variance and prices it in. <strong>PPS</strong> pays the subsidy only and keeps the transaction fees; that was near-irrelevant in 2011 when fees were a rounding error, and expensive later. <strong>PPS+</strong> pays subsidy as pay-per-share and passes fees through on what the pool actually collects. <strong>PPLNS</strong> and OCEAN's <strong>TIDES</strong> pay you only out of blocks the pool really finds, weighted by your recent shares: identical expected return, real variance, and the cheapest fees. <strong>Proportional</strong> and <strong>score</strong> sit further along the same axis, with proportional the most exposed to a long unlucky round.</p><details class="method-detail"><summary>When each scheme was invented</summary><p>Schemes arrived at particular moments rather than always existing, so each pool here carries a dated timeline rather than one fixed method. Proportional came first and paid out whatever the round produced, which made it hoppable; Slush answered that in November 2010 with a time-weighted <strong>score</strong>, and ran it until moving to FPPS on 12 December 2023. <strong>PPLNS</strong> appeared around 2011 and <strong>PPS</strong> shortly after. <strong>FPPS</strong> only became possible once transaction fees were worth passing on, arriving around 2014-15 and becoming the industry default. <strong>PPS+</strong> was ViaBTC's invention in August 2016, and OCEAN's <strong>TIDES</strong> arrived with the pool in November 2023. A pool in this game is never shown running a scheme before it existed, and a regression check enforces it.</p></details><p>The trade-off is live rather than cosmetic. In mid-2023, with transaction fees running about 7% of the block reward, a 0%-fee FPPS pool and a 0%-fee TIDES pool return the same expected BTC per day but the TIDES payout swings roughly fourteen times as widely day to day. Over the same window a 3% PPS pool lands about <strong>9.8%</strong> below the best available option, not 3% - the missing 7 points are the transaction fees it never passes on. A headline fee is not a like-for-like comparison, and the game does not pretend otherwise. A pool that historically shut down stops being selectable once its closure date arrives; if it was your active method when that happens, the fleet fails over to solo mining automatically and the live floor's connection panel reflects the switch.</p>`},
    methodFleetChapter(),
    {id:"method-treasury",title:"Market, treasury and finance",summary:"Prices, venues, fees, cash runway, debt and what happens on settlement day.",lead:"BTC cannot pay a bill. This chapter covers the routes between mined BTC and spendable cash, the fees and counterparty risks on each route, and what the simulation does when the cash is not there at settlement.",body:`<h3 id="method-market">Market, exchanges and venue freezes</h3><p>The game opens tradable BTC/USD on 17 July 2010, when Mt. Gox began continuous trading. That is a deliberate simplification rather than the first time bitcoin had a dollar price: New Liberty Standard published an electricity-cost-derived rate from October 2009, bitcoinmarket.com ran from March 2010, and the 10,000-BTC pizza was May 2010. What those lacked was a continuous order book deep enough to sell a mining operation's production into, which is the thing this game actually models. After that, buying spends liquid fiat and selling converts BTC at the historical price, both after a venue-specific fee; "liquid fiat" is spendable cash only - not BTC, ETF exposure or Lightning liquidity. Exchange, frozen-claim and ETF balances are custodial claims or price exposure, not self-held bitcoin. Mt. Gox and the Frontier venue can suffer historical collapse events that freeze or destroy part of a balance, and any venue carrying a balance can model a temporary withdrawal freeze that blocks sales and transfers without erasing it.</p><p>Four of those collapses had a real, documented public warning first, and the game gives you that warning - but only when you are actually exposed to it. Mt. Gox halted bitcoin withdrawals on 07 February 2014, seventeen days before it went dark; a Canadian bank froze roughly C$28M tied to QuadrigaCX's payment processor, public on 09 October 2018, about four months before that collapse; a leaked balance sheet raised questions about a major venue's sister trading firm on 02 November 2022, nine days before the bankruptcy filing; and China's top financial regulator named Bitcoin mining for the first time on 21 May 2021, about a month before Sichuan's power cuts. Each crossing writes a ledger entry and raises a persistent banner naming the exact balance or region at risk, with one click through to Market or Facilities. The banner is derived live, so it clears the moment you withdraw to zero or relocate away, and retires itself when the dated event arrives. A warning is information only - it changes nothing about the event's effect, which lands in full if you ignore it. Bitfinex's 2016 security loss and Kazakhstan's January 2022 internet shutdown deliberately carry <strong>no</strong> warning, because neither had a genuine public one; inventing advance notice for them would teach false history. Each venue quotes its own bid (what a sell here nets) and ask (what a buy here costs) around the shared historical price, built from that venue's own fee - cheaper venues quote a tighter spread.</p><p>If a monthly settlement can't be covered by cash, choosing to sell BTC does not execute a sale by itself: it opens Market with a live shortfall banner. Covering it means depositing self-held BTC onto a chosen venue and selling it there, at that venue's own quote, through the same confirmation flow as any other trade - the settlement clears the instant cash covers the bill, from any venue, with no cap on how much you choose to sell. Nothing is ever sold for you.</p><h4 id="method-depth">Order-book depth: what a large sale actually fetches</h4><p>A quoted price is what one bitcoin trades at, not what ten thousand of them would. Every market has a finite book, and an order large enough to consume it walks the price down as it fills. The game models this, because without it an early mining fortune is a free one: in December 2010 the entire bitcoin market was capitalised at about $1.3 million, so a well-run 2009 operation's holdings were most of a percent of every bitcoin in existence - an amount that simply could not be sold at the quote, at any price, because there was nobody on the other side.</p><p>The impact you pay is proportional to the fraction of the whole market your order represents, so it depends on the market's size rather than on the date. That matters: the effect needs no era-specific tuning and retires itself. The order that was unsellable in 2010 is a rounding error by 2020, and by the cutoff a thousand-bitcoin sale costs a third of a percent - while a hundred-thousand-bitcoin sale still costs a third of its value, because that is genuinely what dumping six billion dollars into any market does.</p><p>Selling pressure carries between orders and fades over about three days, so slicing one unsellable order into a hundred small ones saves at most half the impact - roughly the advantage real execution desks get - while genuinely waiting for the book to refill works properly. Buying relieves your own selling pressure. The confirmation screen shows the impact before you commit, and the sale you make to cover a bill pays exactly the same impact any other sale would, so routing a liquidation through the month-end bill is not a way around the book.</p><details class="method-detail"><summary>Exact calculation</summary><p>Impact is <code>66 × (standing same-direction pressure + order value) ÷ market capitalisation</code>, ignored below 0.1% and capped at 85%. A sale fills at <code>quote × (1 − venue fee) × (1 − impact)</code>; a buy fills at <code>quote × (1 + impact)</code>. Standing pressure decays with a three-day half-life. Market capitalisation is ${sourceTag("recorded")} - 842 weekly observations of CapMrktCurUSD from the bundled dataset, and past the cutoff it tracks the modelled price, since over 99.5% of the supply is already mined by then. The constant, and the premise that book depth scales with capitalisation, are ${sourceTag("modelled")}.</p></details><h3 id="method-reserve">The reserve: how far away your money is</h3><p>The Treasury tab holds the Market, Custody and Finance as three sections of one place, under a strip that is the same on all three: what you can spend today and for how many days of bills, your hot wallet, your reserve, your custody posture and the next bill. Coins in cold storage are yours and are not spendable this afternoon. Spending them is a signing, which takes days, and the days are the days it takes to fetch the keys a signing needs, so they depend on where you keep them. While the coins travel they are in flight: yours, counted in your net worth, and spendable by nobody. When a bill your cash cannot meet stops the clock, fetching the reserve carries the bill into arrears and starts the signing in one step, so the clock can run and the coins can land. It is refused when arrears are already owed. Rushing a signing halves the days, never below two for a quorum, and pays the day's queue-jumping premium: the 90th-percentile fee rate over the median, between two and six times the ordinary fee.</p><p>The fee is the weight of what is spent, not its value. A sweep weighs ten virtual bytes plus 68 for every coin it gathers from a single-signature wallet or 105 from a quorum, plus 31 for each output, and a virtual byte costs the median rate the day's blocks recorded, never less than one satoshi (outside the record, in the sandbox, it is the fees a block collects divided by a million). Every pool payout you were ever sent is a separate coin, so a low payout threshold in December 2017 is paid for twice, once when it arrives and again when the pile has to be gathered, and a rotation consolidates them into one. The rates are recorded; the byte counts are modelled. Pool payouts and ordinary payments are priced the same way, as about 250 virtual bytes at the day's rate, with one exception: before 2017 fees were a habit and not a market, wallets paid a fixed 0.0002 BTC whatever the network was doing, and the game charges exactly that until the fee market begins. Running a node makes a payment cheaper and a quorum wallet makes it 35% dearer.</p><h3 id="method-payouts">Pool payouts: where income lands</h3><p>A pool's balance is a debt, not your coins: it is owed to you until it clears your payout threshold, which you choose from 0.001 to 0.5 BTC, and then it is sent to the destination you chose, less the network fee. A low threshold pays often and pays a fee each time; a high one leaves more of your money with the pool for longer. Solo mining pays the coinbase of a block you find straight to that destination, with no threshold and nobody who can decide not to pay you. The destination is a choice with a cost whichever you take. Your hot wallet is spendable within the hour and reachable by anyone who reaches the machine. Cold storage needs a wallet that can receive and is days from a bill. An exchange balance is ready to sell and is not yours, and every failure in this game's record began with a balance like it. The payout card says what a reserve built of many small payouts will cost to spend.</p><h3 id="method-finance">Finance: project loans, grid arrears and settlement rescues</h3><p>Project finance draws cash against fiat collateral or six months of recent mining revenue, charging 1.2% monthly interest - 0.9% with a Treasury manager on payroll - settled with the monthly bill. Grid arrears (an unpaid energy, rent or other bill) carry no modelled interest but force an immediate disconnection until paid.</p><p>Nothing is sold on your behalf. Mining pays in BTC and the bill is due in cash, so if cash falls short at settlement the clock stops and you decide how to raise it. An operation that idles and lets a treasury quietly pay every bill is not running a business, so the game does not allow it.</p><p>When a monthly bill exceeds available cash, time pauses for a rescue decision: sell self-held BTC on an exchange (see Market, above); fetch the reserve from cold storage, which carries the bill into arrears so the clock can run while the coins are signed out (see Custody); borrow against your coins without selling them (see Custody), a pledge at once or a quorum loan once its coins have been signed into place; liquidate the least hash-efficient miners first; take emergency bridge finance at a 15% origination premium added to project debt; miss the bill, which carries the shortfall into arrears and keeps the site running until the next bill date; or accept receivership, which seizes 10-25% of self-held BTC and 25% of installed miners (the percentage rising each time) and ends the scored campaign on its third occurrence. Only a clean cash settlement counts as a fully solvent month for the Operator Score - every rescue keeps the run alive but marks that month down.</p><p>Arrears are the one rescue with a deadline of their own. Missing a bill leaves the grid connected until the next bill date, so the operation keeps mining and the next month's costs keep accruing on top. If the arrears are still owed when that date arrives, power and internet are cut: mining and node service stop while rent, payroll, insurance and finance interest carry on. Clearing the arrears from Finance restores service the same day. Only one bill can be carried at a time - you cannot miss a second while the first is outstanding.</p>`},
    {id:"method-keys",title:"Custody and verification",summary:"Keys, hot wallets, cold storage, venues, full nodes, Lightning and counterparty risk.",lead:"Whoever holds the keys controls the bitcoin. This chapter separates BTC you control from BTC someone else is holding for you, and explains what a full node does - and does not - do for either.",body:`<h3 id="method-cold-setup">Setting up cold storage</h3><p>Cold storage is available from the first day, because keeping a key on a computer that never goes online is as old as the software. It takes five steps, in this order, and the <strong>Set up cold storage</strong> card at the top of the Custody section puts the button for the next one on its own line: <strong>get a signer</strong>, <strong>make a key on it</strong>, <strong>write it down</strong>, <strong>put the key in your wallet</strong>, then <strong>move coins in</strong>. Until a wallet can sign, the game will not move coins into cold storage or pay them there, because coins sent to a wallet nobody can sign for are lost.</p><p>The first signer is an old late-1990s PC kept offline: it comes up from the basement for nothing in a day, and there is only the one. If a fire takes it, another can be put together from spare parts, also free and also a day, and the key restored onto it from its backup. Hardware wallets arrive from 2014 and cost more, hold the key more safely and sign faster. Paper is the free backup; it burns with the building it is in, so move it elsewhere in <em>Where things are kept</em> as soon as you can.</p><h3 id="method-hot-grace">The online wallet's first two months</h3><p>The key to the online wallet lives on the mining computer and can be lost, stolen or burnt. Nothing in it can be lost in the first sixty days of a campaign, because a new player has had no chance to learn what a backup is for. After that it carries a small monthly chance of each, which the Custody card states as a yearly figure and which grows with the share of your coins kept there.</p><p>A fire, flood or break-in at the mine also shows on the live mining floor for three weeks (scorch, standing water or police tape), a grid outage turns it dark and an internet outage dims it. That is a picture and changes nothing else. Every custody incident that costs coins ends with its odds: how likely it was in the month it happened, what that is over a year, and, where a precaution exists, what the precaution would have made it. A 1-in-450 month in your first weeks is bad luck; the same loss with most of your coins online is a lesson.</p><h3 id="method-custody">Custody, full nodes and Lightning</h3><p>Hot and cold wallets are the only balances that count as bitcoin you actually control. From 2011, an online hot balance carries a small seeded monthly key-compromise risk that can remove 12–40% of that balance in one incident - cold storage is never touched, and Multisig cuts the odds by 58%. A node does not mine, create BTC or protect exchange balances; it independently validates the chain. Three verification profiles exist once a dedicated ($260) or hardened ($1,200, from 2016) node is bought: Pruned (12 W, $5/month, no Lightning), Archival (25 W, $20/month, enables Lightning and a lower transfer fee) and, on the hardened tier only, Mining relay (120 W, $180/month, faster propagation and routing income). A $2,400 geographic backup node (40 W, $90/month, from 2018) can carry verification through a primary-site outage but can't replace the synchronized primary for local block templates or Lightning. Falling below the modelled chain-size storage requirement takes archival service offline; pruning is the fallback, at the cost of Lightning and the cheaper transfer fee.</p><p>Lightning becomes available from 2018 with an online archival or relay node: it locks hot-wallet BTC as routing liquidity rather than spending it, earning a small daily fee proportional to locked BTC and network activity - Monitoring adds 8% to that fee - paid into the hot wallet, while the principal stays locked until withdrawn.</p><p>Every campaign opens with a one-time ceremony, and the clock does not start until it is done, because a block pays to an address and an address comes from a key: roll dice for entropy, drawn from the browser's real cryptographic random number generator rather than the deterministic seed that drives the rest of the simulation, and the game derives an illustrative private key from the result - a simplification, since a real wallet performs further elliptic-curve math, checksums and address encoding this game doesn't implement. That key is a real key in the game, not a decoration: it is the key behind your online wallet, and the next section says what happens to it. The campaign then starts on whichever wallet client era-fits its start date - the rough original client from 2009, Bitcoin-Qt from 2011, the real Bitcoin Core rebrand from March 2014, and a modern Taproot-ready release from November 2021 - with each later upgrade, once its date arrives, available from the Custody section of the Treasury for a skill point.</p><h3 id="method-signers">Signers, keys and recovery</h3><p>The first key. Before any signer exists there is already one key, the one the opening ceremony made, and it is a software key: a file on the computer that runs the wallet, which is at the mine. It is the key behind the online wallet that every payout lands in until you decide otherwise. It is not part of a wallet policy and cannot be assigned to one, because a policy needs keys on signers of their own, and it is not the reserve. What happens to the mine happens to it. A fire or a flood destroys the computer, a break-in takes it and what it can reach, a seized or stolen backup is a stolen key, and the disk can fail on any month at about 0.15%. The only answer is the answer every key has: a backup that does not share the computer's fate. With one, a lost computer is an afternoon and a fee for fetching the backup; without one, the coins in the online wallet were the file. A lost or stolen wallet is replaced by a fresh key with no backup, and the old one is retired. A run begun from the ceremony has this key and an older save does not, and the game treats the two exactly as it always did. The risk is ${sourceTag("modelled")}.</p><p>Three separate things, and keeping them separate is the point. A <strong>device</strong> is an object you own. A <strong>key</strong> is a secret generated on one. A <strong>wallet</strong> is a policy saying which keys may spend. Buying a device protects nothing at all - it protects something once a key exists on it and that key is assigned to a wallet. Three devices restored from the same seed are one key, and the wallet counts them that way, because a 2-of-3 built from a single seed is a single-signature wallet in three enclosures.</p><p>Signers can be bought or built. Commercial devices arrive from a named vendor, which means that vendor holds a record of the purchase. A <strong>SeedSigner</strong> is assembled from generic single-board-computer components - a Raspberry Pi Zero v1.3, a camera, a 240x240 display with controls and a microSD card, with an optional printed enclosure - bought from suppliers who have no idea what you are building. That reduces how much bitcoin-specific information a purchase leaves behind. It is not anonymity, and the game does not pretend otherwise. A SeedSigner is also <strong>stateless</strong>: it holds no seed between uses, so losing the device is not losing the wallet as long as the seeds and the wallet configuration are still recoverable.</p><h4 id="method-multisig">Multisig is a trade, not an upgrade</h4><p>Two risks pull against each other, and choosing between them is the whole decision.</p><table><tr><th>Risk</th><th>What happens</th><th>What answers it</th></tr><tr><td>Compromise</td><td>Somebody else spends your coins</td><td>A spending quorum. One stolen secret out of three moves nothing.</td></tr><tr><td>Loss</td><td>Nobody can spend them, including you</td><td>Backups - of every seed, and of the wallet configuration.</td></tr></table><p>A 2-of-3 cuts the compromise risk hard. It also gives you three things to keep instead of one, and a quorum wallet needs its <strong>descriptor</strong> as well as its seeds: the seeds alone cannot rebuild it. A multisig whose configuration has never been written down is <em>more</em> likely to strand your coins than a single-signature wallet with one good backup, and that is not a contrivance - it is a way real people have really lost real bitcoin. Record the configuration and the same wallet becomes the safest thing on the page.</p><details class="method-detail"><summary>Exact calculation</summary><p>Compromise risk is multiplied by x1 with no configured wallet, x0.55 for a configured single-signature wallet and x0.18 for a configured quorum. The monthly chance of an unrecoverable loss starts at 0.16% for a configured wallet, x0.35 when every assigned key has a backup, x0.45 again when every backup is on durable media, and then x0.3 for a quorum whose configuration is recorded - or <strong>x2.4 for one whose configuration is not</strong>. Key identifiers in this game are fictional labels; no recovery phrase, real or simulated, is ever shown. All of these figures are ${sourceTag("modelled")}.</p></details><h4 id="method-entropy">When the device itself is the problem</h4><p>A leaked customer list is a privacy failure. A defective key is a different kind of event entirely, and the game models both because they are not the same thing and should never be confused.</p><p>Coinkite has disclosed that a configuration error introduced with a March 2021 firmware migration caused some COLDCARD devices to fall back on a software random number generator instead of the hardware entropy source when generating a seed. Reported analysis put the resulting key strength as low as 40 bits rather than 128 - weak enough to search. Thefts began on 30 July 2026; Galaxy Research's running tally reached roughly 1,816 BTC, about $116M, from more than 5,200 addresses across four waves, with the first wave moving around 594 BTC in twenty-five minutes. Those figures are ${sourceTag("recorded")} as reported, and attributed rather than presented as the game's own.</p><p>The important property, and the reason this fits the model rather than sitting beside it, is that <strong>weakness belongs to the seed and to the moment it was generated</strong>. It is not a property of the device you are holding now. A firmware update - in Coinkite's own words - "corrects future seed generation but does not repair an existing affected seed". Move that seed to a different signer and it is still weak. The only remedy is a new seed and a migration, and the game gives you exactly that remedy and no other.</p><p>So a single-signature wallet on an affected seed is swept. A wallet on a seed from any other source is untouched. And a 2-of-3 holding one affected key among three <em>survives</em>, because a key somebody can guess still cannot meet a quorum on its own - which is the defence in depth that independently generated keys on independently designed devices are for. Two affected keys in the same quorum, and it opens.</p><p>The waves take a share of what you hold each day for as long as the wallet can still be opened by guessable keys alone. Rotating to a fresh key stops it immediately. Whether you can rotate at all depends on whether you already own another signer, which is the argument for having one before you need it.</p><h4 id="method-supplierleak">What a vendor breach does and does not mean</h4><p>On 29 July 2020 Ledger disclosed that an unauthorised third party had accessed its e-commerce and marketing database, exposing contact details for around a million subscribers and order information for a smaller group of customers. The company stated that no payment details and no device secrets were involved, and that the devices themselves were unaffected. That is a ${sourceTag("recorded")} disclosure and this game does not embellish it.</p><p>What follows here is a ${sourceTag("modelled")} consequence, and the distinction matters. A leaked customer list does not let anyone spend your coins and is not evidence that a device was compromised. What it produces is targeted attempts - convincing impersonations of the vendor, asking you to re-enter a seed somewhere you should not. If your wallet is a quorum, an attempt that succeeds in extracting one secret still spends nothing. If it is single-signature, it can cost you.</p><p>Exposure attaches to the purchase, not to the product category: it reaches devices bought from that vendor inside the window the disclosure describes, and leaves every other owner alone. It also does not expire. Buying a different device afterwards does not withdraw a record that has already left the building.</p><h4 id="method-places">Where the keys are kept</h4><p>A backup is only a backup if it does not share a fate with what it backs up. Every signer, seed backup and copy of the wallet configuration is kept in a place: the mine, home, a bank deposit box or a trusted person's house. Each place trades the same three things. <strong>Distance</strong>: a key in a bank box is a trip away, and the days to fetch the keys a signing needs are added to the signing. <strong>Fate</strong>: each place has a modelled monthly chance of a fire, a flood and a break-in, highest at the mine and lowest in a bank. <strong>Cost</strong>: a deposit box is billed monthly.</p><p>Fire and flood destroy signers and paper; steel survives them. A break-in takes whatever is there, steel included, and a stolen seed backup is a seed somebody else now holds: enough of them is the coins, and fewer is a key to replace. If a signer is destroyed but its backup survives, the key is restored onto a new device. If every copy of what a wallet needs was in the place that burned, the coins are still on the chain and nobody can move them. The descriptor of a quorum is not secret and can be copied, which is what lets a 2-of-3 survive losing the place that held the first copy.</p><p>Moving something takes days, and while it travels it cannot sign. So does fetching a backup: restoring a key from one kept in a bank takes the two days the bank takes, and from the mine none, so the safest place is the slowest to recover from and a fire just before a bill makes the distance matter. Anything kept at the mine goes with the fleet when it moves region, and a border is a place things can be stopped. A bank box is in a country, and a state that bans your business can open it: when China named mining a prohibited business in June 2021, a mine in Sichuan could lose everything it kept in a box, steel included, and enough seeds in it is a theft that cover will not pay for. A box in any other country is untouched. The incident rates are modelled and meant to give roughly one event per run at the mine; they are not historical.</p><h4 id="method-people">Who holds a key</h4><p>A key is a secret, and a secret somebody knows leaves with them. A key can be held by you or by the treasury manager, a security officer or a field technician, and dismissing the person who held it does not remove the key from the wallet: it makes it known to someone who no longer works for you. Every key a person ever knew is exposed when they go, even one handed back, because nobody forgets a seed. A technician is one of several, so a dismissed technician exposes a key in proportion to how many there were.</p><p>An exposed key raises the wallet's compromise risk until it is replaced, and it stays usable: you can keep signing with it, and the danger is the clock. If the exposed keys alone are enough to satisfy the wallet, somebody else can spend, and the chance that they do is rolled every day and rises every day. Half of those who learn a key and leave mean to use it, and within a month it is more likely than not; the rest are patient, do nothing for at least three months, and then their chance climbs too. The game does not say which one you have. A security officer halves the chance. One exposed key of a 2-of-3 is harmless; the same key in a single-signature wallet is the whole wallet.</p><p>Replacing a key is a rotation, and it is a job, not a click. A new key is generated on a spare signer, every coin is swept to the new wallet at the real fee for the weight of the coins, and it takes days during which the wallet cannot be spent from. It consolidates the coins into one, which is the cheapest thing to spend next time, and it invalidates the written descriptor of a quorum, which has to be recorded again. A stolen backup and a seed with a known weakness are replaced the same way. A rotation can be rushed: half the days, never fewer than two because a quorum cannot be fully rushed, for that same premium on the sweep fee. The signer that held a retired key still holds it, so it cannot take a new key until it is wiped, which resets the device and leaves the seed backup alone. A technician who is busy on a repair crew is a day later to sign.</p><h4 id="method-credit">What lenders and insurers see</h4><p>Anybody who prices a risk on your keys reads one word, your custody <strong>posture</strong>. <em>None</em>: no wallet that can sign, or one that could not be rebuilt if a signer were lost. <em>Basic</em>: it can sign and be rebuilt. <em>Strong</em>: nothing about it is a known weakness, so no key anybody else knows, no seed with a known flaw, the places recorded with no single one holding everything, every backup on steel and enough working signers. <em>Audited</em>: strong, and your security officer has had it independently looked at within the last year. An audit costs $4,000 and takes fourteen days, reports every finding whether or not it passes, and a pass is a certificate for a year that a worse wallet does not keep.</p><p><strong>Coin cover</strong>, from 2016, pays when coins are stolen: half of a covered theft at a basic posture, 70% at strong, 85% audited, in cash, and the better the posture the cheaper the premium. It pays for the online wallet being emptied and for a break-in. It does not pay for a seed you knew was flawed, a seed you typed into a fake, or a key you knew somebody else held and had not replaced; nor for a venue failing, nor for coins nobody can spend any more; nor inside the first thirty days.</p><p>From mid-2018 you can borrow against the coins instead of selling them. In <strong>collaborative custody</strong> the lender holds one key of your 2-of-3 and cannot move the coins by itself: it lends 50% of their value at 0.7% a month, needs a strong wallet, and the coins are swept into the wallet it co-signs over a few days at the real fee. In a <strong>full-custody pledge</strong> you send the coins to the lender at once from the hot wallet: it lends 40% at 1.0% a month, needs nothing of your custody, and the coins are a claim on a company. Either way the loan is a fixed sum against coins that move. You choose how much of what the coins allow to borrow, and borrowing less is the only way to make a loan safe: a loan at the limit was sold within a year in a sixth to a third of the weeks since 2018, and one at 60% of it in about a fiftieth. A loan gives notice five points before it is called. Past 75-80% of their value the lender calls it and you have two weeks to repay it or add coins; past 85-90%, or when the two weeks are up, it sells enough to clear the debt and a 5% penalty, however low the price is. A pledge goes to a named lender, picked for you: Celsius, Voyager and BlockFi stopped paying out on 12 June, 1 July and 10 November 2022, and Nexo and Ledn did not, and from outside they looked the same. Only the lender that failed takes a pledge, and it leaves the debt, which becomes ordinary borrowing; a quorum loan has no lender to lose, because the one that held a single key of three cannot touch your coins. You can repay a quarter, half or all of a loan, and the same share of the coins comes back at the same loan to value. Pledged coins count as yours and cannot be spent.</p>`},
    {id:"method-advance",title:"Progression and scoring",summary:"Operator XP, knowledge, skills, milestones, eras and the Operator Score.",lead:"Operating well earns experience, knowledge and skill points, and those unlock lasting capabilities rather than instant multipliers. This chapter covers how each accumulates and how the final Operator Score is built from them.",body:`<h3 id="method-xp">Operator XP, levels and the best share</h3><p>A permanent experience bar in the header tracks how much operating you have actually done. XP comes from four places, all of them real mining telemetry or real work: <strong>shares submitted</strong> each day, <strong>new best-share difficulty records</strong>, <strong>machines commissioned</strong>, and <strong>repairs completed</strong> - with a 50% bonus on a repair you carried out yourself rather than handing to a crew.</p><p>Share XP is deliberately <em>logarithmic</em> in hash rate, not linear. A fleet spans eleven orders of magnitude between a 2009 laptop and a modern megacampus, so a linear reward would pay the opening years nothing and the closing years absurdities. In practice a laptop earns about 8 XP a day and a 324 PH/s campus about 51 - roughly a six-fold spread across that whole range, which keeps the bar meaningful in every era. The share count itself is modelled the honest way, as <code>hash × 86,400 ÷ 2³²</code> difficulty-one shares per day.</p><p>The <strong>best share</strong> figure beside the bar is the one number solo miners actually watch: the highest-difficulty share your fleet has ever found. It is drawn from a heavy-tailed distribution around your daily share count, so it climbs in rare jumps rather than smoothly, and beating your own record by a wide margin is announced. It is a personal record, not a block - finding it changes nothing about your payout.</p><p>Level <em>N</em> needs <code>60 × (N−1)²</code> cumulative XP, so early levels arrive in days and later ones take seasons. Levels grant skill points on a decelerating schedule - every level to 6, then every second level to 20, then every fourth - which works out at roughly 22 points across a long campaign, against the 80 needed to buy the entire tech tree. A full historical run played actively lands somewhere around level 55.</p><p>XP <strong>augments</strong> the Operator Score rather than replacing it, and the distinction matters: XP only ever goes up, so on its own it would reward sitting still for seventeen years. Peak level therefore contributes a capped 60 points of <em>mastery</em> (reached at level 50), taken out of the era-performance allocation, which now carries 740 rather than 800. The remaining 200 still come from milestones, holdings, reserves and resilience, and the Score keeps every one of its penalties for bridge loans and receivership. Operating well and operating long are scored as two different things.</p><h3 id="method-progression">Learning, skills and operating strategy</h3><p>One forum post, book or podcast occupies the single learning slot at a time; some finish with a knowledge check, and crossing a knowledge threshold - like major story events and mining milestones - awards a skill point. The first threshold is 5 knowledge, and every threshold after it is three further knowledge, so the Tech tree opens early in a run and then compounds steadily. Skills change real calculations rather than adding score directly: Compute skills touch hash and hardware cost, Energy skills touch power cost, Operations skills touch pool fees, uptime, relocation and connectivity risk, and Treasury skills touch loan and custody-transfer economics. Power contracts trade a cheaper spot-market rate for exposure to energy shocks; staff add recurring salary for a targeted benefit, and field technicians specifically are the input that keeps fleet wear and repair times down. Any role can be dismissed again from the Finance desk: salary stops immediately and one month's notice is added to that month's accrued bill, so hiring a crew for a single job and letting them go is not free. A technician already committed to a running repair cannot be dismissed until that job clears, and losing your last technician switches auto-repair back off and returns you to the bench.</p><h3 id="method-risk">Optional risk and civil-society actions</h3><p>Historical-era speculative bets risk a chosen slice of hot-wallet BTC with deterministic, loss-biased outcomes. Donations send a chosen portion of hot-wallet BTC permanently. Neither is required to finish the game.</p>`},
    {id:"method-sources",title:"Recorded history and sources",summary:"The bundled datasets, how values between anchors are derived, and where events come from.",lead:"This is the provenance chapter. Recorded values come from the bundled historical dataset. Derived values are calculated from recorded inputs, usually by interpolating between anchors. Modelled values are gameplay assumptions. Anything not described here as recorded is one of the other two.",body:`<h3 id="method-events">Historical events and sources</h3><p>Event cards mix protocol, mining, custody, policy, adoption and market-structure milestones. Each shows a modelled 30-day BTC price-path comparison as context only, not a causal claim; a small set of dated events changes gameplay directly - venue failures, regional shutdowns - and major events can award skill points.</p>${recordedMetricSnapshot()}`},
    {id:"method-sandbox",title:"Procedural sandbox",summary:"What continues after the recorded history ends, what stops, and which values become modelled.",lead:"Past the historical cutoff the simulation keeps running on projections. This chapter is the line between the two: what the sandbox refuses to invent, what it models deterministically, and what keeps happening because the protocol says so.",body:`<h3 id="method-sandbox-model">What the sandbox invents, and what it refuses to</h3><p>The bundled historical record ends on ${dateFmt(END)}. Continuing past that point starts the procedural sandbox: the same operation and the same rules, for up to 100 further simulated years - but every input is now a projection rather than a recorded value.</p><h4>What stops at the cutoff</h4><p>No new historical chapters and no new hardware releases exist after ${dateFmt(END)}, because nothing real exists to add. The story feed goes quiet and the hardware catalogue stops growing: the newest machine available on the last recorded day is the newest machine you will ever buy. Nothing the game shows you after the cutoff is recorded history.</p><h4>What continues as a model</h4><p>Price, network hash rate, network difficulty, transaction activity, transaction fees, chain size and block height all continue. Each is a deterministic function of the date alone - the same date always produces the same value, and the live gameplay random number generator is never consulted, so re-rendering a screen can never change a projected figure. Price follows a decaying growth trend anchored to its last recorded value, shaped by a halving cycle measured from the history you have just replayed: detrending each complete recorded epoch and averaging the log residual by phase gives a trough just after the halving, a peak about a third of the way through, a long drawdown and a recovery into the next one. Its amplitude starts below the historical figure, because the most recent epoch was already far flatter than the first, and decays as the asset matures. The wobble around it is sized from recorded volatility, which fell from roughly 139% annualised across 2011-13 to 46% across 2023-26, and continues that decay. Hash rate follows the same cycle with a lag and a third of the swing, because capacity is ordered after a rally and lands months later; difficulty follows hash rate; block height advances at a constant ten-minute interval. These are bounded projections built to keep operating decisions meaningful. They are not forecasts.</p><h4>What continues as protocol</h4><p>Bitcoin's issuance schedule is not a projection. The subsidy halves every 210,000 blocks and keeps doing so. What is projected is <em>when</em>: the sandbox assumes a constant ten-minute block interval, so each halving lands about 3 years and 11 months after the last. Real block intervals vary, so treat every projected halving date as modelled and the direction as certain.</p><p>The first projected halving falls in ${dateFmt(nextHalvingTime(END),true)} and takes the subsidy from ${fmtSubsidy(subsidyAt(END))} to ${fmtSubsidy(subsidyAt(nextHalvingTime(END)))} per block. Twenty-five projected halvings occur before the sandbox ends. Each one is recorded in your Ledger and announced as a <strong>Projected protocol halving</strong> - never as a historical chapter - and each is a reason to re-check your mining margin, because the same hash rate earns half the subsidy it did the day before.</p><h4>Whole satoshis</h4><p>Bitcoin has no unit smaller than a satoshi, and the subsidy is halved by integer division. ${fmtSubsidy(subsidyAt(END))} is ${Math.round(subsidyAt(END)*1e8).toLocaleString("en-US")} satoshis; every halving floors the result rather than keeping a fraction. From the tenth halving onward that flooring is visible - 4,882,812 satoshis rather than 4,882,812.5 - and this simulation floors it the same way, so no payout, forecast or displayed value ever contains a fraction of a satoshi. By the end of the sandbox the projected subsidy is ${fmtSubsidy(subsidyAt(SANDBOX_END))} per block, and transaction fees dominate mining revenue completely.</p><h4>The endpoint</h4><p>The sandbox is finite. It ends 100 years after the historical cutoff, on ${dateFmt(SANDBOX_END)}, and there is no further continuation to accept: the run closes with the same final recap as the historical ending.</p>`}
  ];
}
